152 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 99 0 R/T(Village Vistas: "Heavenly" Red at the Playground)/E(Village Vistas: "Heavenly" Red at the Playground)/Pg 10 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

153 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 99 0 R/E(If you have taken a stroll past the Lakewood Park playground this January, your eyes have likely been drawn to a spectacular splash of color against the winter grey. While our deciduous trees are bare and the grass is recovering from the cold, one shrub is putting on a firework display of bright red berries and bronze-tinted foliage.)/Pg 10 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

154 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 99 0 R/E(We\220ve heard the debates on the sidewalk: Is it bamboo? Is it a young tree? Is it our native holly? The answer is Nandina domestica, widely known as Heavenly Bamboo. While it is a common sight in gardens and park strips, this plant has a fascinating story \(and a few secrets\) that you should know.)/Pg 10 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

155 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 99 0 R/E(An Imposter in the Bamboo Family)/Pg 10 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

156 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 99 0 R/E(Despite its common name and its upright, cane-like stems, Nandina is not a bamboo at all. It is actually an evergreen member of the Barberry family. It earned the nickname "Heavenly Bamboo" because of its resemblance to tall grass and its reputation in East Asian culture as a plant that dispels bad dreams and brings good luck. Here in Lakewood, it is prized for its toughness, because it is incredibly drought-tolerant and pest-resistant.)/Pg 10 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

157 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 99 0 R/E(The Winter Transformation)/Pg 10 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

158 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 99 0 R/E(Nandina is a master of seasonal disguise, which is why it grabs so much attention in January.)/Pg 10 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

159 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 99 0 R/E(Spring & Summer: The leaves are a glossy, soft green, often with small white flowers.)/Pg 10 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

160 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 99 0 R/E(The January Shift: As the temperature drops, the plant pumps anthocyanins \(red pigments\) into its leaves to protect them from the cold. This turns the delicate, lacy foliage into stunning shades of purple, bronze, and fiery crimson.)/Pg 10 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

161 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 99 0 R/E(The Berries: The bright red clusters you see now actually ripened in late fall, but they are incredibly persistent. They can hang onto the branches for months, providing long-lasting color when almost nothing else is blooming.)/Pg 10 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

162 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 99 0 R/E(Safety for Pets)/Pg 10 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

163 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 99 0 R/E(A quick reminder for our dog-walking neighbors: All parts of the Nandina plant are toxic to domestic pets if chewed on. While most dogs ignore it, keep curious puppies away from those low-hanging red berries during your park loops.)/Pg 10 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

